# Release manifest. One file per release; never edit a published one, add a new .<n> instead.
SWIFT_VERSION=6.4.2
RELEASE_ID=6.4.2-hyp3r.5
BASE_IMAGE=swift:6.4.2-noble
BASE_IMAGE_SLIM=swift:6.4.2-noble-slim
LIBDISPATCH_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift-corelibs-libdispatch
LIBDISPATCH_REF=44cf5cea488db0f5b0541347be8c7a4ed4aec73b
FOUNDATION_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift-corelibs-foundation
FOUNDATION_REF=b790123c442bb0dfde0b4c51edae1f3d1f87996c
SWIFT_FOUNDATION_TAG=swift-6.4.2-RELEASE
SWIFT_FOUNDATION_ICU_TAG=swift-6.4.2-RELEASE
SWIFT_COLLECTIONS_TAG=1.1.6
FIXES="Swift 6.4.2 with the hyp3r fixes. SC-0001 (libdispatch epoll use-after-free, swiftlang/swift-corelibs-libdispatch#949) is in official 6.4.2 (upstream backport #971, identical to our patch), so it is no longer patched here. SC-0002: Foundation Process on Linux over-reads dirent.d_name in findMaximumOpenFromProcSelfFD, intermittent SIGSEGV (swiftlang/swift-corelibs-foundation#5472); backport of upstream commit 81eb85a2 onto swift-6.4.2-RELEASE. SC-0004: the optimizer moved a class field-offset load above the allocation of the first instance (metadata accessors are modelled as readnone), so the first object of a class with a non-constant layout could get a field store over its isa pointer, intermittent SIGSEGV or silent heap corruption in -O builds; fixed in swift-frontend (LLVM alias analysis of swift_allocObject), the Linux images and tarballs carry the rebuilt compiler SC-0006: #isolation in nonisolated(nonsending) code (the default under NonisolatedNonsendingByDefault) ignored its contextual type, e.g. #isolation === MainActor.shared, so -O builds aborted in the LLVM verifier ('PHINode should have one entry for each predecessor', swiftlang/swift#92798); now converted, and types (any Actor)? cannot convert to are diagnosed like in @MainActor code (#isolation != nil there becomes an error; write (#isolation as (any Actor)?) != nil). SC-0019: #isolation in nonisolated(nonsending) code was consumed without a copy, so e.g. String(describing: #isolation) over-released the calling actor (deinit while running, then SIGSEGV; swiftlang/swift#92799). Both fixed in swift-frontend; the Linux images and tarballs carry the rebuilt compiler. SC-0005: a typed-throws closure passed to a generic untyped-throws parameter (e.g. Task { () async throws(E) -> T in ... }) crashed the compiler for error types with storage and returned a corrupted error for small enums (swiftlang/swift#92312, a 6.4 regression); fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0003: -O builds of async functions that take a small enum or struct whose native explosion is coerced through a stack temporary (e.g. an enum with a tuple payload) could lose their spilled self and arguments: IRGen sized the temporary's lifetime markers in bits instead of bytes, so after the temporary moved into the async frame the optimizer deleted the spills, and the first resume read garbage (SIGSEGV or wrong values); backport of upstream swiftlang/swift#91312 (main only), fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0022: a typed-throws closure whose context type is a type parameter (stored in [() throws(E) -> T], passed through a generic id<T>) crashed the compiler in SILGen; fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0014: on Linux a DispatchSource signal source never fired once the signal had been set to SIG_IGN (or SIG_DFL) again after the first source for it registered (libdispatch installed its sigaction only once per signal, so the kernel discarded the signal); libdispatch now reinstalls its handler on every registration when the disposition is SIG_IGN/SIG_DFL (fix in src/event/event_epoll.c, not upstream yet); also in the static Linux SDK's libdispatch.a. SC-0023: our SC-0006 fix made every call that passes #isolation to an isolated parameter in nonisolated(nonsending) code count as an isolation crossing, so valid code that stock Swift accepts was rejected ('non-Sendable ... returned from actor-isolated ... to nonisolated context', e.g. for await over non-Sendable elements, Swift Testing's #expect(processExitsWith:), or 'cannot be called from outside of the actor' for synchronous callees); a regression of hyp3r compilers since Linux hyp3r.9 / macOS hyp3r.8, fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0024: with the SC-0001 libdispatch, SwiftPM's plugin runner kept reading a plugin's stdout/stderr pipes at end-of-file in a tight loop (one CPU busy after every plugin run, e.g. +68% CPU per re-plan); usr/bin/swift-package is rebuilt from the patched SwiftPM fork (swift-build, swift-test, swift-run are symlinks to it). sourcekit-lsp links SwiftPM statically and is not rebuilt. SwiftPM: the full Linux images carry the official swift-syntax 604.0.0 prebuilts (swift.org has none for 6.4.1; the 6.4.0-RELEASE files are used, stored under this image's own compiler tag) in /opt/hyp3r/swiftpm-prebuilts, so macro packages that depend on swift-syntax 604.0.0 skip compiling swift-syntax: swift build --experimental-prebuilts-download-url file:///opt/hyp3r/swiftpm-prebuilts (also in HYP3R_SWIFTPM_PREBUILTS_URL). SC-0031: on Linux the libdispatch workqueue monitor timer woke every process once a second for its whole lifetime, also when idle with no worker threads left (battery cost for always-on services); it now runs only while a monitored worker exists, in the shared and the static libdispatch and in the static Linux SDK. Marker string hyp3r-dispatch-workq-monitor-idle-v1 (grep -c) in libdispatch.so, libdispatch.a and statically linked binaries. SC-0032: in actor- or global-actor-isolated code an optional-chained mutation whose later parts await (dict[k]?.x = await f(), ?.x += await f(), ?.append(await f()), also through a non-Sendable class the actor holds) began its access before the await and held it across the suspension, so concurrent tasks on the actor lost their updates and, with class payloads, could hit a use-after-free or SIGSEGV, with no diagnostic; the compiler now warns (diagnostic group ActorIsolatedOptionalChainSuspension, a warning in Swift 5 and 6 modes, promote with -Werror ActorIsolatedOptionalChainSuspension); fix: move the await into a local first. SC-0034: with -sanitize=thread, SILGen put TSan inout instrumentation on object projections (the load_borrow of a ~Copyable self, struct_extract), which kept SILGenCleanup from narrowing a let read to the stored property, so legal disjoint accesses (inner.add(&box.value)) were rejected as overlapping; the builtin is now emitted only for addresses; fix in lib/SILGen/SILGenLValue.cpp, not upstream yet. SC-0033: SwiftBuild (the default SwiftPM build system) decided whole-module optimization from build settings only, so a release target that turned WMO off with .unsafeFlags([-no-whole-module-optimization]) on top of the SwiftPM release default -Owholemodule got WMO outputs planned while the driver compiled non-WMO, and the build failed with unable to open dependencies file (Target-primary.d); the reverse (-wmo in a debug build) failed the same way. The last WMO flag in OTHER_SWIFT_FLAGS now decides; usr/bin/swift-package is built against Hyp3rSon1X/swift-build hyp3r/release/6.4.2 (not upstream yet). SC-0035: -O and -Osize builds of a ~Copyable struct whose mutating method calls a throwing or rethrows function crashed the compiler with 'SIL verification failed: read-only scope invalidated by a local write' once that method was inlined (the move-only address checker destroyed the inout field in the dead-end error block); backport of upstream swiftlang/swift#90163 (main only), fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0036: on Linux x86_64, -Onone code crashed with signal 11 (an aligned movaps on an 8-byte aligned stack temporary) when a value with 16-byte alignment that is passed or returned directly, e.g. a struct or enum holding SIMD4<Float> or UInt128, was stored into a local or an array literal (also swiftlang/swift#93129); IRGen now aligns the forwarded coercion temporary to the Swift type; fix in lib/IRGen/IRGenSIL.cpp, not upstream yet. Changes over 6.4.2-hyp3r.4: SC-0035 and SC-0036 added (compiler), nothing else. Over 6.4.1-hyp3r.5: the base moved to swift-6.4.2-RELEASE (its own optimizer, IRGen and runtime fixes included), SC-0001 dropped (upstream), SC-0033, SC-0034, SC-0035 and SC-0036 added."
# Patched Static Linux SDK (musl): the official bundle below with libdispatch.a and libFoundation.a rebuilt from the refs above.
STATIC_SDK_VERSION=0.1.0
STATIC_SDK_URL=https://download.swift.org/swift-6.4.2-release/static-sdk/swift-6.4.2-RELEASE/swift-6.4.2-RELEASE_static-linux-0.1.0.artifactbundle.tar.gz
STATIC_SDK_SHA256=3786b179936d9cc9e266dd506bd1654176358d7b25288e243359f4d17cc4dc5a
# Compiler overlay (SC-0003, SC-0004, SC-0005, SC-0006, SC-0019, SC-0022, SC-0023, SC-0032, SC-0034, SC-0035, SC-0036): swift-frontend rebuilt from the ref below plus the unmodified swift-6.4.2-RELEASE
# LLVM/Clang/cmark/swift-syntax/string-processing trees, release build without assertions, swapped into the official toolchain.
# The static SDK's own libraries do not depend on the compiler fix; code built against it still uses the host compiler.
SWIFT_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift
SWIFT_REF=7988d244532f2533937f918aab492af9eefb23d1
SWIFT_UPSTREAM_TAG=swift-6.4.2-RELEASE
# SwiftPM overlay (SC-0024, SC-0033): usr/bin/swift-package rebuilt from the ref below (swift-6.4.2-RELEASE + SC-0024) against the
# deps in overlays/swiftpm/deps-6.4.2.txt (swift-build: our fork with SC-0033), recipe in overlays/swiftpm.
SWIFTPM_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift-package-manager
SWIFTPM_REF=2290f88f8394906211f66444a9872c2921efb2e7
# SwiftPM swift-syntax prebuilts baked into the full images (jobs image, tests/prebuilts-swift-syntax): the official swift.org
# files for SWIFTPM_PREBUILTS_SOURCE_TAG, stored under the compiler tag the built image reports (computed in the build).
# swift.org publishes none for 6.4.1 or 6.4.2; the 6.4.0 files are used (same swiftmodule format, gate-tested).
SWIFTPM_PREBUILTS=swift-syntax:604.0.0
SWIFTPM_PREBUILTS_SOURCE_TAG=swift-6.4.0-RELEASE
